WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.560
Back in June at AI DevCon in London,
somebody finally said the thing out loud.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.640
Almost every demo

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.200
you see starts with an empty repository,
even though nobody actually works in one.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.160
Let's first hear from Katie Roberts,

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.880
technical director at Nearform,
who would like us to stop calling legacy

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:19.920
code a big ball of mud
and start thinking of it as a city.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.040
I'm Katie Roberts.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.200
You might recognize me from such tracks
as the latent space,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.240
where I've been emceeing for the last day
and a half, and I'm here

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:31.920
to talk to you about how you can stop
maintaining and start evolving.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.960
Applying AI native
engineering in brownfield code bases.

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.920
Developers send more time
deciphering the code and fixing bugs

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:43.680
and resolving issues than implementing
new features and improvements.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.400
Small updates become really difficult,
change becomes hard.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.160
Future development starts to crawl.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.640
Anyone who stuck in this space
as well becomes very demotivated.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.680
And it's nobody's fault
because every decision on this code

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:58.560
base was made
with the best possible intentions.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.880
Nobody wants the code to get like this,

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.880
and it's sometimes referred to
as the ball of mud pattern.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.960
I don't think of brownfield code
as a ball of mud.

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:09.600
I think of them much more like a city.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:11.560
A city is

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:14.640
very successful, and it's something
that's evolved over time.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.320
And brownfield systems
tend to work like this.

00:01:17.320 --> 00:01:18.800
They work incredibly well.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:22.160
They have huge numbers of customers
working on them,

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.200
and that is why they've been around
for five, ten, 15 years

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.320
while maintaining complex user
demands, while at the same time working

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:36.080
through problems like performance,
security and scaling that come with that.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.640
And like the city,
they've evolved as a response to the needs

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.920
and there are areas
in that functioning city.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:44.080
So this is actually London.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.760
I took this last weekend
from one of the top of the skyscrapers.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.880
I'm very pleased with this picture.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.640
It's lovely, but you can see on this
you've got paths through the city

00:01:51.640 --> 00:01:52.960
which are very, very clear.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:53.480
These are

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:57.080
these are the well-trodden paths, the ones
that we all know where it's going.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.200
And then there are less
well maintained spaces, dead ends

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.360
and the places that you really wouldn't
want to get caught in alone late at night.

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.120
So this is like your code base,
and you want to evolve it into something

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:12.600
that is easier to build
on, that's more understood,

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.120
and you get that high level view
so you can start working through.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:19.640
We also heard from Simon Martinelli,
who does modernization

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.640
for a living
and is blunt about what it isn't.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:26.760
Translating COBOL into
Java is not modernization.

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.080
That is lift and shift.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:33.760
What he does instead is reverse engineer
the specification back out of the code,

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.800
get the business to check it, and generate
from there.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:37.120
that.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.800
What I do
mostly is software modernization.

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:45.000
So I'm doing modernization for enterprise
applications for about eight years now.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.560
And there I just changed the process.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:53.680
That means I extract

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:56.920
use case and entity model
from code and tests and documentation.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:00.520
Maybe I have confidence here or whatever,
because usually the documentation

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:04.800
is spread around
multiple artifacts that we have,

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.080
and then we have the entity model
and the use cases.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:10.600
They will be revised
or reviewed by the business people.

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:15.320
And then we generate the new code,
because there are a lot of IDs

00:03:15.320 --> 00:03:19.080
that we can directly transform,
maybe from COBOL to travel.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.600
So that's also something
that anthropic is, is telling us.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.080
But that never worked.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:30.000
So we did that like 30 years ago
COBOL to see or call to C++ or something.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.400
But that's not helpful
because that's just lift and shift.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.440
And modernization is not lift and shift.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:39.840
Modernization is rethinking
how people are working

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:43.640
with the software, integrating features
that maybe are not there.

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:47.920
And because of this reverse engineering,
we also have

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:53.240
a positive feedback from the end users
because we are not transforming

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:59.120
from one technology to another, because
regarding our use case and entity model.

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:02.800
And we can integrate new features
because usually you don't do that.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.240
So the guideline when we started
with the modernization project

00:04:07.280 --> 00:04:11.120
two years ago was
we want to have the exact same system

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:12.400
just in another technology,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.280
and you don't add new features because
we don't want to introduce new bugs.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.520
And now we can just do that
because we just change the specification.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.240
Christopher Batey has a warning

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:27.440
about something you've just inherited
without noticing your harness,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.600
your model hosting, and your model are
three boxes you don't control.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.920
And if your provider goes down
at the same moment as your production

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:38.920
outage, and that model is the only way
your engineers know how to debug.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:40.480
Good luck.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:45.200
The next thing I wanted to dig
a bit deeper into is have a real

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.280
think about the dependency you want to put
on the producer of your digital product.

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:52.440
So if we go back to this diagram
and we've accepted that,

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.680
maybe we don't understand
how agent workflows actually produce code.

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.880
If we were to break it down a bit more.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.320
So I'm breaking down the producer box
into three things.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.360
We've heard the first one,
I've called it interface,

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.920
but in most places we'll call it harness,
like in the keynote.

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:08.640
So there's an interface for how you do
that, right?

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:10.800
That could be Claude Code.
It could be OpenCode.

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:11.720
It could be Codex.

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:16.760
It could be some automatic workflow
based off your, your your GitHub issues.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.280
Then you've got the thing
which is hosting your model. Right.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:21.600
So that could be directly
it could be the anthropic API,

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:23.240
but you could be hosting your own.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.440
You could be using a third party
which reached between them.

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:27.960
And then you've got your model.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:31.040
So really your black box is made up
of three things that you don't understand.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:31.800
It's called Claude Code.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.680
It's an API which perhaps at 330 on
an afternoon stops working.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.640
Anyone experienced that?
Does anyone feel the most productive?

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:41.720
Once the Americans wake up and
and make the anthropic APIs.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:42.480
Go down?

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:45.120
And then you've got the model,
which obviously you don't understand.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.320
I'm not saying you only see on this day,
if anyone wants to explain

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.200
how opus works to me,
I'm very happy to hear it.

00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:55.040
So we can choose not to be quite
so coupled to that workflow.

00:05:55.040 --> 00:05:57.400
We can use a harness
which is open source.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:05:59.040
What's the benefit of that?

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.680
You know, when on profit goes down,
I just switch to open API

00:06:01.680 --> 00:06:03.160
with a single command and I carry on.

00:06:03.160 --> 00:06:04.720
I obviously get different results.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:08.040
Every model gives you different results,
but it's a good to have the way

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:11.040
that you're building your digital product
be able to flip between providers.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:13.800
You could go as far as to hosting
the models themselves.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:14.640
That limits the models.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.320
You could do use something like so,

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.320
and then if you want,
you could use a model

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:22.680
with open weights or at least an open
source way, the way you that's trained.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.520
I don't think any of that
is that important, apart from being able

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.120
to switch between providers very quickly.

00:06:28.120 --> 00:06:31.680
If you've got a production outage
and suddenly at the same time, let's say

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.520
your favorite AI provider does,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:36.640
and the only way your engineers can debug
production is with that model.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:38.800
Good luck. anticlockwise.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:42.200
Ian Thomas at Meta
on about the least inviting brownfield.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:46.120
There is low level VR code on one

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.400
very powerful windows
machine full of patterns.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.320
Nobody wanted to go near.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:55.040
One engineer taught the agent
how he would do the refactor,

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.680
found the same shape all over the code
base and became the reviewer instead.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:00.960
Refactoring in

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:04.080
VR means that you're going to be spending
a lot of time in your windows

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:07.840
PC looking at some pretty gnarly low level
code at times.

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:09.160
And there's a lot of legacy patterns

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:11.680
that we'd built up over the years
that needed to be refactored out.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:13.760
And people
tended to shy away from these things

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:16.160
because there's a higher risk
of breaking things.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.880
You know, these are the refactorings
and not something that you would undertake

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.160
for fun.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.240
But one of the engineers thought,
okay, well, if I can teach the AI

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.520
the way I would think about
resolving these refactorings and then go

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.280
and find patterns that are similar
across the code base, what can we achieve?

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:39.360
And again, roughly
half the time spent in achieving his goal.

00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:43.720
But he went out and found multiple areas
where this pattern could be reapplied

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:44.800
and set the agents off

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:47.800
in a kind of team pattern,
so they would go and work in parallel.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:49.480
And then he was actually
more as a reviewer

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.800
and just being a critical
architectural oversight,

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:53.360
making sure
that it was doing the right thing

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.600
and not making any kind of gross
miscalculations

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.200
on how we actually wanted the
system to look afterwards.

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:02.080
And moving on from that, like I said,

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:07.200
we had a challenge with VR in that you're
kind of constrained to one machine.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:11.320
But one of the engineers
who was thinking about this from the

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:14.680
what is the what is the machine actually
need to render and what what can we do

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:19.120
that doesn't require us to have
this massive PC with a huge GPU in it?

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:23.840
If we provide better context
to the models when they're doing work,

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:27.600
would they be able to work in our more
standardized on demand worlds?

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.520
And the result of this was an MCP server

00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:35.520
that was a bridge
between the data that backs Horizon World.

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:38.800
So all of the things that explain
how world state looks,

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:40.160
how how things are built,

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:43.240
the models, the kind of textures
and everything that are in the worlds,

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:46.000
it taught the models
exactly what it needed to know

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.440
about how horizon works,
so they could go and work largely

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:53.400
in isolation
and in multiple on demand environments.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:57.280
And so that removes the whole windows
PC barrier.

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:01.000
And it meant that we could parallelize
the work in a lot more effective way.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:04.040
And again, the AI was cleverer.

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:08.040
It knew more about the the world that
we were working in than it did before.

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:10.000
So we got better outputs as well.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.800
So not only was it more efficient
and we could parallelize more,

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.520
we actually found that
the quality of the work went up to.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:16.840
Thanks for watching.

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:21.520
Be sure to join us for the next
AI DevCon this November in New York City.

